      "chapterTitle": "I. Movement One: The Wada of 1938",
      "heading": "I. The Sagwan Latch in Sadashiv Peth",
      "body": "The iron ring latch on the main door of the Kulkarni Wada had been forged in the summer of 1938 by a village blacksmith from Saswad.\n\nIt was not a modern cylinder lock designed for quick departure. It was a heavy, cold loop of black country iron, pitted with small hammer blows, secured to the three-inch-thick Sagwan teak leaf by four square-headed rivets that had oxidized into a dull, velvety plum color over eighty-seven monsoons. To open it, you had to lift the ring with two fingers, turn it forty-five degrees counter-clockwise until the internal cam cleared the jamb, and lean your shoulder into the wood to overcome the seasonal swelling of the timber.\n\nJayant Kulkarni had performed that exact sequence of physical motions every morning of his life since his seventh birthday.\n\nHe performed it at six-twenty on a Thursday in late January, stepping out into the narrow lane of Sadashiv Peth with his canvas satchel slung across his chest and the kick-starter pedal of his 2004 Bajaj Chetak scooter waiting on the cobblestones.\n\nThe wada rose three storeys above him into the cool Pune dawn—a monumental, listing galleon of grey basalt foundation stone, sun-dried brick, and dark Deccan timber. In 1938, when his grandfather Dattatreya Kulkarni had supervised the masonry while working as an assistant clerk in the district collectorate, the house had been surrounded by open guava orchards and the slow, clean waters of the Mutha river. Grandfather Dattatreya had kept a small ledger bound in parchment, written in the fast, cursive loops of Modi script, recording every rupee spent on the construction:\n\n*Bhadrapada, 1938: Twelve carts of basalt stone from the quarry at Sinhagad — ₹48.*\n*Ashwin, 1938: Four beams of Dandeli seasoned teak, transported by railway to Pune station — ₹112.*\n*Kartik, 1938: Brass hinges, iron latches, and wages for eight stone-masons for three weeks — ₹64.*\n\nFor eighty-seven years, that basalt and teak had absorbed the lives of three generations.\n\nNow, the wada was suffocated on three sides by five-storey concrete residential complexes with names like Sai Krupa Residency and Shree Ganesh Heights, their grey split-air-conditioner outdoor units dripping oily condensate onto the grandfather's red Mangalore roof tiles. The lane outside, once wide enough for two bullock carts to pass under the shade of tamarind trees, had been squeezed into a narrow asphalt trench where two hundred scooters revved their engines at eight in the morning, their handlebars scraping against the carved stone plinths of the old houses.\n\nInside the house, the silence had a specific, dense texture.\n\nFor thirteen years, that silence had not existed. For thirteen years, between June 2011 and October 2024, the ground-floor bedroom behind the courtyard had been an outpatient hospice ward. It had smelled of Dettol, boiling rice congee, rubber catheter tubing, eucalyptus oil, and the sweetish, terrifying rot of Stage III sacral bedsores.\n\nNow, their mother Malati was gone.\n\nShe had passed away three months earlier, on the ninth day of Navratri, slipping away between four and four-thirty in the morning while Jayant was downstairs heating water on the single-burner kerosene stove to sponge her back. When he had carried the brass basin into her room, her hand had already fallen over the edge of the mattress, the fingers loose and curved like dry leaves, pointing toward the faded linoleum floor.\n\nJayant had not cried that morning.\n\nHe had set the brass basin down on the wooden stool, covered her shoulders with the white khadi sheet, and stood by the barred window listening to the milkman's bicycle bell ring down the lane.\n\nBehind him on the stone terrace, his younger brother Vivek was now awake.\n\nVivek had arrived the previous evening on the nine-fifteen IndiGo flight from Hyderabad, carrying a rimless titanium laptop sleeve, an insulated stainless steel coffee flask, and a navy Samsonite hard-shell suitcase that looked as though it had never touched unpaved ground.\n\nHe was thirty-eight years old—four years younger than Jayant—with the sleek, athletic posture of an executive who paid eighty-five thousand rupees a year for an gym membership in Gachibowli, drank black Americanos at his desk, and measured his waking hours in fifteen-minute calendar increments.\n\nJayant watched his brother come down the timber stairs.\n\nThe wooden risers groaned under Vivek's sneakers—a sharp, hollow creak that Jayant knew by heart. Step four was loose; step nine had a cracked brass bracket that their father Shrikant had reinforced with two iron screws in the winter of 1996.\n\nVivek walked into the courtyard, holding his phone in his left palm like an external organ, his thumb flicking across the glass display with mechanical velocity.\n\n'Jayant-bhau,' Vivek said in English, then checked himself and shifted into colloquial Marathi. 'Sub-Registrar office Swargate... appointment is for ten-thirty, right? Shinde-ji from Surya Developers texted. He said if we reach by ten, he will keep the token numbers ready.'\n\nJayant looked at his brother's clean-shaven face, at the faint purple shadows under his eyes, at the expensive tailored grey linen shirt that smelled faintly of cedarwood and airline air-conditioning.\n\n'Yes,' Jayant said softly, adjusting his silver wireframe spectacles. 'Ten-thirty.'\n\n'Good,' Vivek nodded, glancing up at the timber eaves where two pigeons were rustling in the carved woodwork. 'Let us finish the registration before lunch. The developer has already transferred the twenty percent advance into escrow. Once the signature deed is executed, the RTGS will hit both accounts by four in the afternoon.'\n\nHe said it briskly, casually, the way he would announce the closing of a convertible debt note to his co-founders in Hyderabad.\n\nJayant did not answer.\n\nHe walked to his scooter, turned the fuel petcock to Reserve, and kicked the starter pedal with his heel.",
