      "id": "sec-br-3",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II. The Thirteen Years of Bedsores",
      "heading": "II. The Thirteen Years of Bedsores",
      "body": "In the middle-class mythology of Maharashtra, the elder brother is expected to be the banyan tree: he stays near the roots, he absorbs the drought, he shelters the elderly, and he surrenders his personal velocity so that the younger children may fly across oceans.\n\nNobody mentions the smell of the room.\n\nIn June 2011, when their mother Malati suffered the massive right-hemisphere ischemic stroke that destroyed her speech center and paralyzed her left side, Vivek had just completed his Master's degree at the University of Texas at Austin. He was twenty-five years old, holding an offer letter from a cloud infrastructure firm in Seattle with a starting compensation package of one hundred and twelve thousand dollars.\n\nJayant had been thirty-two, teaching high school chemistry at Bhave High School for a monthly salary of twenty-eight thousand rupees.\n\nJayant remembered the transatlantic phone call on the night of the stroke.\n\nHe had stood in the cold hallway of the Sassoon General Hospital, beside a red fire extinguisher with an expired inspection tag, holding the receiver of a public coin telephone against his ear while the diesel ambulances reversed into the casualty bay below.\n\n'Bhau,' Vivek had wept from Austin, his voice breaking across the satellite delay. 'Should I come back? Tell me, I will cancel the Seattle joining. I will book the first flight to Mumbai.'\n\nJayant had looked through the scratched glass door of the intensive care unit.\n\nInside, their mother lay with three translucent plastic tubes taped to her nostrils, her grey hair matted with sweat, her chest hitching with each mechanical stroke of the ventilator. Beside her bed stood his wife Rohini, twenty-eight years old, married to him for less than two years, holding a stainless steel kidney tray filled with vomit.\n\nJayant had taken a deep breath.\n\nHe had felt the monstrous, crushing weight of the older brother's script settle onto his collarbones like a yoke of lead.\n\n'No, Vivek,' Jayant had said into the receiver, his voice calm, flat, deliberate. 'Seattle is your career. You worked four years for that visa. If you come back now, what will you do in Pune? Sit in Sassoon hospital? Mother cannot speak anyway. I am here. Rohini is here. Go to Seattle.'\n\nVivek had cried with gratitude. 'I will send money every month, Bhau. Every single dollar. Whatever the treatment costs, don't compromise on anything.'\n\nAnd Vivek had kept his word.\n\nFor thirteen consecutive years, Vivek had sent money.\n\nFirst six hundred dollars a month from Seattle; then eight hundred; then, after he returned to India in 2018 to launch his startup in Hyderabad, forty thousand rupees on the first of every calendar month by automated NEFT transfer into Jayant's State Bank of India savings account.\n\nWhenever an emergency occurred—a urinary tract infection requiring five days in the ICU at Deenanath Mangeshkar Hospital, a new motorized air-bed to relieve sacral pressure, an imported pulse oximeter during the second wave of COVID—Vivek had transferred the required amount within sixty seconds via UPI or net banking.\n\nHe never questioned an invoice. He never complained about the cost.\n\nOn paper, Vivek was the model NRI son: dutiful, generous, prompt, carrying the financial responsibility of an ailing mother while conquering the global technology frontier.\n\nOn paper, everything was balanced.\n\nExcept that an NEFT transfer cannot turn an eighty-kilogram paralyzed woman every two hours between midnight and six in the morning to prevent necrotic tissue from rotting against the bedsheets.\n\nA net-banking transaction cannot change an adult diaper soaked in liquid diarrhea at three-fifteen on a Tuesday morning before you have to stand in front of forty-five tenth-standard boys to teach the periodic table.\n\nA credit card cannot sit beside a feverish old woman who has forgotten your name and hold her cold, trembling fingers while she screams in terror at the shadows cast by the ceiling fan.\n\nRohini had done that. Jayant had done that.\n\nFor four thousand seven hundred and forty-five days.\n\nJayant had watched his wife's youth evaporate in the ground-floor room of the wada.\n\nRohini had been a girl with a master's degree in Marathi literature, a girl who loved the poetry of Kusumagraj and had dreamed of directing student plays at the Tilak Smarak Mandir. Instead, her twenties and thirties had been consumed by the laundry of incontinence: washing three sets of bed linen every single morning in cold water, boiling rubber draw-sheets with bleaching powder, measuring sodium valproate drops with an eyedropper, massaging coconut oil into bedsores that wept clear, yellow serous fluid through sterile gauze.\n\nHer hands had become rough, calloused, permanently smelling of liquid chlorhexidine.\n\nMother had lost the power of speech, but she had developed a private code of eye movements: one slow blink meant she needed water through the bent plastic straw; two blinks meant her lower back was burning; three rapid blinks meant she wanted the window opened so she could hear the bells of the Omkareshwar temple across the river.\n\nIn 2017, when their daughter Sanika was seven years old, the child had written a school essay titled My House.\n\nJayant had kept that folded sheet of lined paper in his desk drawer:\n\n*In my house there is a big door and lots of wood. In the bottom room my Aaji sleeps. Aaji does not walk. My mother gives Aaji medicine. Sometimes my house smells like the hospital. My father is always washing clothes on the stone.*\n\nWhen Jayant had read those sentences, he had closed the drawer and sat alone in the chemistry staff room for forty minutes with his head on his desk, his spectacles resting beside a jar of copper sulphate crystals, weeping in silence.",
