      "id": "sec-br-4b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II-B. The Oxygen Cylinder of April 2021",
      "heading": "II-B. The Oxygen Cylinder of April 2021",
      "body": "If there was one night that permanently broke something inside Jayant, it was the fourteenth of April, 2021.\n\nIt was the peak of the second wave of COVID-19. Pune had become a cemetery of sirens. Every hospital from Sassoon to Ruby Hall had put up handwritten cardboard notices outside their emergency gates: *No Beds. No Oxygen. Do Not Enter.*\n\nAt ten o'clock that night, Malati's oxygen saturation dropped to seventy-one percent.\n\nHer lips had turned a terrifying, bruised blue, her ribcage heaving like a landed fish, her eyes rolling upward toward the timber ceiling. The rented oxygen concentrator in the corner had begun wheezing, its digital display flashing an error code: *Compressor Failure — Service Required.*\n\nJayant had called Vivek in Hyderabad.\n\nVivek was frantic. He had spent three hours on Twitter and WhatsApp, tagging government ministers, startup founders, venture capitalists, and pharmaceutical executives. Within forty minutes, Vivek had found an emergency contact for an industrial supplier in Chakan who had five medical oxygen cylinders available for black-market purchase at sixty-five thousand rupees each.\n\nVivek had transferred the money via Google Pay instantly.\n\n'Bhau,' Vivek had shouted into the phone, his voice shaking with hysteria from his air-conditioned apartment in Gachibowli. 'The cylinder is booked! Supplier name is Mahendra Shinde, Plot 42, Bhosari Industrial Area. Send someone with a tempo immediately!'\n\n'There are no tempos, Vivek,' Jayant had said, his voice terrifyingly quiet. 'The police have imposed a strict night curfew. Nobody is on the road.'\n\n'Hire an ambulance! Pay them twenty thousand!'\n\n'There are no ambulances, Vivek. People are carrying dead bodies in the trunks of auto-rickshaws.'\n\nJayant had put the phone down on the wooden table.\n\nHe had walked into the courtyard, taken the empty ten-liter steel oxygen cylinder from the corner—forty-two kilograms of cold, dead iron—and lashed it between his knees on the floorboard of his Bajaj Chetak scooter with two nylon washing lines.\n\nHe had strapped his helmet on, kissed Rohini on her cold forehead, and kicked the scooter to life.\n\nFor twenty-four kilometers, in the middle of a midnight curfew, Jayant had driven that sputtering 2004 two-stroke scooter through the darkened streets of Pune.\n\nAt the Khadki police checkpost, three constables in riot gear had stopped him with raised lathis. When they saw the empty cylinder between his legs, when they saw the hospital discharge summary taped to his chest, the older head constable had lowered his stick, looked Jayant in the eyes, and waved him through without a word.\n\nHe had reached the industrial shed in Bhosari at twelve-forty.\n\nThere had been eighty men waiting in the dirt under a halogen floodlight—sons, husbands, fathers—each clutching an empty iron cylinder, their faces hollow with the primal madness of men whose families were suffocating at home.\n\nJayant had stood in that queue for three hours.\n\nWhen the heavy, frost-covered cylinder of compressed oxygen was finally loaded onto his scooter floorboard, his arms were shaking so violently that he could barely twist the throttle.\n\nHe had driven back through the dawn mist, his knees bruised black and blue by the iron cylinder banging against his patellas over every pothole on the Old Pune-Mumbai highway.\n\nHe had reached the wada at five-fifteen in the morning.\n\nRohini was sitting on the floor beside the bed, holding Malati's hand, reciting the *Gajendra Moksha* stotra with cracked lips.\n\nJayant had connected the brass regulator with an adjustable spanner, cracked the valve open, and watched the green ball inside the flowmeter rise to four liters per minute.\n\nWithin twelve minutes, the blue color had drained from his mother's lips. Her chest had settled into a slow, rhythmic rise and fall.\n\nJayant had walked out to the courtyard stone, sat down beside the well, and vomited his stomach bile into the drain.\n\nVivek had called at six-thirty.\n\n'Bhau! Thank God!' Vivek had cried, weeping with relief. 'My Twitter network worked! I told you, Bhau, money and contacts can solve any crisis in India!'\n\nJayant had held the phone against his ear, listening to his younger brother celebrate in Hyderabad.\n\nHe had looked down at his bleeding knees, at the oil-stained cuffs of his trousers, at the bruise where the iron valve had gouged his flesh.\n\n*Yes, Vivek,* Jayant had thought, not uttering a single word aloud. *Your Twitter network worked. Your sixty-five thousand rupees worked. But you were not the one holding forty kilograms of iron between your knees while the police pointed sticks at your head.*\n\nThat was the night the ledger between the brothers had ceased to be an financial account and had become an open, unhealing wound.",
