      "id": "sec-br-6",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "III. The Hyderabad Transfers and the Dollar Ledger",
      "heading": "III. The Hyderabad Transfers and the Dollar Ledger",
      "body": "Vivek Kulkarni sat on the stone ledge of the wada well, looking at his iPhone screen.\n\nHis fingers tapped the glass with nervous, restless velocity. He opened his banking application, entered his biometric Face ID, and stared at the current account balance of his company, LogiSync Technologies Pvt Ltd:\n\n*Available Balance: ₹4,18,920.40*\n\nIn twenty-two days—on the fifteenth of February—his payroll was due.\n\nLogiSync employed twenty-two software engineers, data scientists, and operations leads in an open-brick office space in Hitec City. His monthly salary obligation was twenty-eight lakh sixty thousand rupees.\n\nIf the funding winter had not frozen the venture capital ecosystem in mid-2024, Vivek would never have returned to Pune to sell the house.\n\nHe had raised a seed round of one point two million dollars in 2021 during the peak of the post-pandemic digital boom. He had been featured in YourStory and Inc42; his photographs had shown a smiling thirty-five-year-old founder in a dark hoodie standing before a whiteboard covered in neural network architecture.\n\nHe had believed—with the absolute, intoxicating arrogance of the tech ecosystem—that he was building a generational enterprise.\n\nThen the global interest rates had climbed. The Silicon Valley venture funds had retreated into defensive crouches. His Series A term sheet from a Singapore-based venture fund had been abruptly cancelled after twelve weeks of due diligence. For six months, Vivek had survived on bridge loans, credit lines, and delayed vendor payments. Three months ago, without telling his wife Anjali, he had pledged their personal four-BHK apartment in Gachibowli as collateral for a seventy-five-lakh-rupee working capital overdraft with a private bank.\n\nIf he could not inject liquidity into the company before mid-February, the bank would declare the overdraft in default, the engineers would walk out, his reputation in the Hyderabad startup circuit would be pulverized, and his home would be auctioned under the SARFAESI Act.\n\nHe was drowning.\n\nAnd the only life raft left on earth was his grandfather's Sagwan timber and stone wada in Sadashiv Peth.\n\nHe looked up at the timber balconies.\n\nTo Vivek, the wada was not a temple of ancestral memory. It was an albatross.\n\nIt was an obsolete, decaying, termitic pile of damp bricks that consumed eighty thousand rupees a year in structural repairs, had no car parking, leaked whenever the monsoon rainfall exceeded forty millimeters in twenty-four hours, and kept his brother trapped in the nineteenth century.\n\nHe loved Jayant. He genuinely, deeply respected Jayant.\n\nWhenever he thought of his brother, Vivek felt a tight, suffocating knot of guilt in his chest. He knew that Jayant had stayed so that he could leave. He knew that Rohini had sacrificed her youth so that Anjali could attend yoga retreats in Goa and their twins could study in an international school with an Olympic-sized swimming pool.\n\nHe had tried to compensate with money.\n\nWhenever Jayant had sent a message saying that Mother needed a specialist consultation in Mumbai, Vivek had sent fifty thousand rupees. Whenever the hospital bills arrived, he had paid them within an hour.\n\nIn Vivek's mind, money was not an insult; it was the highest form of sacrifice he could offer from five hundred miles away. He worked eighty hours a week, ruined his sleep with cortisol, neglected his own health, and took terrifying entrepreneurial risks to generate the capital that sustained the family.\n\nWhy did Jayant always look at him as if the money were contaminated?\n\nWhy did Jayant receive every bank transfer with that cold, tight-lipped, formal courtesy—*“Received thirty thousand rupees, thank you, Vivek”*—that made Vivek feel like a visiting stranger paying a hotel bill?\n\nVivek stood up from the well ledge, pacing the narrow stone courtyard.\n\n'Bhau,' Vivek called toward the kitchen. 'Should we leave? Shinde-ji just called again. The registrar takes lunch at one o'clock. If our file isn't scanned before twelve-thirty, we will be stuck there until four in the evening.'\n\nJayant emerged from the kitchen holding a faded canvas cloth bag tied with blue thread.\n\nHe was wearing his best formal clothes: a light grey safari suit made of polyester-cotton blend that he had stitched for a cousin's wedding in 2016, his black leather sandals freshly polished with brown nugget paste, his hair neatly parted with coconut oil.\n\n'I am ready,' Jayant said quietly.\n\nHe did not take his scooter.\n\nHe locked the heavy Sagwan door, checked the iron latch twice with his shoulder, placed the brass key into his pocket, and walked out toward the rickshaw stand at the corner of Bajirao Road.",
