      "id": "sec-br-8",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "IV. Movement Two: The Sub-Registrar's Office at Swargate",
      "heading": "IV. The Queue on the Third Floor",
      "body": "The Sub-Registrar's Office for Haveli Taluka No. 4 was situated on the third floor of an administrative complex near the Swargate bus terminus.\n\nIt was ten-fifteen in the morning, and the building had already reached a state of thermal and auditory saturation.\n\nThe air smelled of hot transformer oil, damp cement dust, stale cutting chai, betel leaf spit dried to a dark maroon crust on the stairwell landings, and the sour sweat of two hundred citizens gathered to surrender or acquire title to the earth of Maharashtra.\n\nEvery wall was lined with steel Godrej almirahs, their olive-green paint peeling like sunburned skin, tied shut with frayed coir rope to prevent thousands of decaying land record dockets from spilling onto the grey terrazzo floor. Ceiling fans with rusted iron down-rods wobbled dangerously overhead, cutting through the humid air without cooling it, merely circulating the dry paper dust of eighty years of property transactions.\n\nIn the hallway, deed writers sat behind portable folding tables with manual Godrej typewriters, their inked ribbons striking green bond paper with a continuous, metallic clatter: *tak-tak-tak-tak-ping*. Beside them stood stamp vendors with wooden cash boxes, selling non-judicial stamp papers embossed with the state emblem, their thumbs blue from counting five-hundred-rupee adhesive revenue stamps.\n\nBeside them on wooden benches sat families from the rural talukas of Haveli, Junnar, and Shirur: old farmers with white Gandhi topis clutching cloth bundles of land revenue receipts dating back to the British survey of 1941; young IT couples in denim jackets buying twenty-five-lakh-rupee flats in Hinjewadi; widows in maroon nine-yard saris accompanied by suspicious sons-in-law eyeing the mutation extracts.\n\nThere was an argument occurring near Counter 3: two cousins from Kothrud were screaming at each other over an eight-inch strip of boundary wall between two family plots, their lawyers brandishing photocopied mutation registers from 1974 while a constable leaned against the wooden railing, chewing roasted betel nut with bovine indifference.\n\nJayant watched the two cousins shouting.\n\nHe saw in their contorted faces the terrible, inevitable disease of Indian family real estate: the ancestral poison that turns brothers into litigants, turns childhood bedrooms into court exhibits, and reduces decades of shared milk and bread to affidavits filed under Section 52 of the Transfer of Property Act.\n\nHe had promised himself, when their mother had died, that he would never stand in this corridor shouting at Vivek over square feet.\n\nIf the house had to go, it would go with dignity. It would not be drowned in the venom of lawyer's notices.\n\nShinde-ji was waiting for them near the water cooler.\n\nHe was a short, barrel-chested man of fifty with dyed jet-black hair, a crimson tikka on his forehead, three gold rings on his right hand, and a laminated developer's identity badge pinned to the pocket of his striped bush-shirt: *Surya Developers & Builders Pvt Ltd — Liaison Executive.*\n\n'Aao, aao, Kulkarni-sir,' Shinde-ji beamed, rushing forward to shake Vivek's hand with excessive, theatrical warmth. 'Everything is prepared. Token number forty-two. I have already verified the 7/12 extract, the death certificates of Shrikant-pant and Malati-tai, the CTS map from the city survey office, and the non-encumbrance certificate from 1950 to 2024.'\n\nHe tapped a two-inch-thick green legal file clutched under his arm.\n\n'Surya-sir himself was asking about you, Vivek-sir,' Shinde-ji said in an oily, confidential whisper. 'He knows your startup in Hyderabad. He told me: *Kulkarni-sir is a modern businessman, he understands velocity. Don't make him wait in line with the farmers.*'\n\nVivek smiled—a tight, professional grimace that signaled compliance while preserving executive distance.\n\n'Thank you, Shinde-ji. Let's finish the process quickly. I have an investor call at three.'\n\nShinde-ji turned toward Jayant, his smile dimming by several watts as he registered the older brother's safari suit, the canvas cloth bag, and the silver wireframe spectacles.\n\n'Namaskar, Jayant-sir,' Shinde-ji said with perfunctory respect. 'You have brought the original death certificate of Malati-tai? And your Aadhaar card and PAN card?'\n\n'Yes,' Jayant said.\n\nHe did not smile. He held his canvas bag tightly against his ribs, standing with his feet together on the dusty terrazzo tiles, looking at the door of the Sub-Registrar's cabin.\n\nAbove the door, a red LED counter displayed the current token: 38.\n\nFour numbers remained.\n\nThirty minutes of legal existence before the Kulkarni Wada ceased to belong to the descendants of Dattatreya Kulkarni and became *Survey No. 412/B, Final Plot 88, redevelopment parcel under Regulation 33(7) of the Pune Unified Development Control Regulations.*",
