      "id": "sec-br-10",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "V. Forty-Four Signatures on Green Bond Paper",
      "heading": "V. Forty-Four Signatures on Green Bond Paper",
      "body": "At eleven-forty, Shinde-ji ushered them into the inner registration chamber.\n\nThe Sub-Registrar, a heavy-set man in his late fifties with gold-rimmed spectacles and a safari suit identical in cut to Jayant's, sat behind a chipped laminate desk piled high with computer printouts. Beside him on a rusted metal bracket sat the equipment of digital governance: a web camera mounted on a gooseneck arm, a flatbed optical biometric fingerprint scanner, and an inkjet printer that wheezed rhythmically as it printed revenue receipt slips.\n\n'Token forty-two,' the registrar said without looking up from his tea glass. 'Kulkarni wada, Sadashiv Peth. Developer sale deed. Both co-owners present?'\n\n'Both present, saheb,' Shinde-ji said eagerly, placing the green file on the desk and sliding an open packet of mint lozenges toward the clerk. 'Elder son Jayant Shrikant Kulkarni, school teacher. Younger son Vivek Shrikant Kulkarni, CEO from Hyderabad. No other legal heirs. All encumbrances clear.'\n\nThe registrar flipped open the file.\n\nThe pages were heavy, thick, pale-green legal bond sheets embossed with the lion capital of Ashoka and printed with violet judicial stamps of five hundred rupees each.\n\n'Read the execution clause,' the registrar ordered, pointing a blunt, ink-stained fingernail at page eighteen.\n\nVivek leaned over the desk. His eyes skimmed the dense, archaic legal syntax of the Maharashtra Land Revenue Code:\n\n*“...the Vendors do hereby grant, convey, transfer, and assure unto the Purchaser all that piece and parcel of freehold land bearing CTS No. 1104, admeasuring 4,200 square feet, together with the three-storey brick and timber residential superstructure standing thereon, commonly known as Kulkarni Wada, absolutely and forever, free from all encumbrances, charges, liens, mortgages, and claims of whatsoever nature...”*\n\n'Forty-four signatures each,' the clerk instructed, handing them two pens.\n\nVivek took his pen—a matte-black rollerball from his shirt pocket—and began signing with rapid, practiced elegance: *V. S. Kulkarni*.\n\nSign on page 4. Sign on page 7. Sign on page 11 at the margin. Sign on page 16 beside the boundary schedule. Sign on page 22 across the joint indemnification warranty.\n\nTo Vivek, each signature was a step toward solvency.\n\nWith each flourish of black ink, the crushing debt in Hyderabad receded by a fraction of an inch. Each signature represented ninety thousand rupees of runway for his company. Each signature pushed the threat of public bankruptcy further into the distance.\n\nBeside him, Jayant did not use the developer's pen.\n\nHe reached into his canvas bag, pulled out an old black Parker Vector fountain pen that his father had gifted him upon completing his B.Ed in 1998, unscrewed the cap, and examined the nib.\n\nHe lowered the pen to the green paper.\n\nHis hand was completely steady, but he moved with agonizing slowness.\n\nOn page 4, his signature touched the boundary description: *North by Sadashiv Peth main lane, South by Joshi property, East by open well.*\n\nHe remembered falling into that open well in the monsoon of 1989 when he was ten years old. He had slipped on the wet basalt stone while trying to rescue a plastic cricket ball, splashing into eight feet of cold, subterranean water.\n\nIt was his father who had jumped in after him—shoes, trousers, leather watch and all—grabbing him by the collar of his school shirt and treading water for fifteen minutes until the neighbours lowered a coir rope.\n\nHis father's watch had stopped at four-fifteen that afternoon. It had never worked again.\n\n*Jayant Shrikant Kulkarni.*\n\nHe signed on page 7, beside the schedule of built-up area: *Ground floor four rooms, timber colonnade, central chowk.*\n\nIn the smallest of those four rooms, his mother had spent the last four thousand days of her life.\n\nHe remembered the sound of the plastic suction machine at two in the morning. He remembered the yellow plastic mug he used to pour warm water over her hair on Sundays. He remembered the exact spot on the lime-plaster wall where she had scratched three vertical lines with her thumbnail during the fever of 2016, trying to count the days until Diwali.\n\n*Jayant Shrikant Kulkarni.*\n\nHe signed on page 16, across the demolition authorization clause: *“...the Purchaser shall have the unrestricted right to raze, dismantle, demolish, and remove the existing superstructure...”*\n\nWith those words, the Dandeli teak pillars would be sawn into timber scrap. The stone chowk where his grandfather had sat on winter mornings reading the Kesari newspaper would be scooped out by a JCB excavator to make room for eight basement parking slots.\n\nThe wada would not merely cease to belong to them.\n\nIt would be eradicated from the surface of the earth.\n\n'Jayant-bhau,' Vivek whispered, his voice tight with impatience, noticing that Jayant was only on page twelve while he had already finished all forty-four. 'Sign quickly, please. The registrar has another file waiting.'\n\nJayant looked at his brother.\n\nHis eyes through the silver wireframe spectacles were dark, deep, entirely unreadable.\n\n'I am signing, Vivek,' he said softly. 'Eighty-seven years took time to build. Give me three minutes to sign it away.'",
      "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1589829545856-d10d557cf95f?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=85",
      "alt": "A person signing a legal document with a fountain pen beside official government seals",
      "caption": "Page sixteen, the demolition clause. Three minutes to sign away what took eighty-seven years to build.",
      "imageSize": "medium",
      "imageWidth": 1600,
      "imageHeight": 1067
