      "id": "sec-br-18",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "IX. The Forty Boxes in the Attic",
      "heading": "IX. The Forty Boxes in the Attic",
      "body": "By five in the afternoon, the winter twilight had settled over the tiled roofs of Sadashiv Peth like a layer of blue dust.\n\nThey mounted the third-floor staircase together, carrying two rolls of brown packing tape and six bundles of flat-folded corrugated cardboard boxes that Jayant had purchased from a wholesaler near Appa Balwant Chowk.\n\nThe attic had not been opened since their father's death in 2004.\n\nIt was a cavernous, low-ceilinged timber loft supported by heavy rafters of unpeeled teak trunk, smelling of dry wood rot, dried neem leaves used to deter silverfish, eighty years of coal smoke from the kitchen chimney, and the powdery musk of decomposing newsprint.\n\nThrough the gaps in the timber shutters, slanting bars of amber sunlight illuminated columns of floating golden dust.\n\nVivek sneezed twice, coughing against the dryness.\n\n'Where do we start, Bhau?' he asked.\n\n'Father's survey maps,' Jayant said, pointing toward three long zinc cylinders resting on an iron shelf against the masonry chimney. 'And the books.'\n\nThey knelt side by side on the bare floorboards.\n\nFor three hours, without turning on the electric bulb, moving by the fading natural light of the Deccan sky, the two brothers emptied the attic.\n\nInside the first wooden chest were their father's surveyor instruments: a brass Gunter's measuring chain with heavy oval links; a boxwood folding rule marked in inches and chains; two sighting compasses in scarred leather holsters; and his hand-drawn maps of the postal routes connecting Satara, Kolhapur, and Sangli, drawn on tracing linen in waterproof Higgins black ink.\n\nEvery map was signed at the corner with their father's minute, flourishing signature: *S. D. Kulkarni, Assistant Surveyor, Posts & Telegraphs, Govt. of India.*\n\n'Do you remember his bicycle?' Vivek asked, holding the brass compass in his palm. 'The black Raleigh with the leather frame bag?'\n\n'He rode forty kilometers a day on that cycle,' Jayant said, wrapping the brass compass carefully in old newspaper. 'When he returned on Saturday evenings, his knees would be swollen. Mother would boil neem leaves in water and tie hot compresses to his joints.'\n\nIn the second chest were their own childhood artifacts.\n\nA wooden cricket bat with a split willow blade, repaired with three brass screws and wrapped in red electrical insulation tape; Vivek's ninth-grade geometry compass box with the chipped chrome plating; Jayant's Fergusson College chemistry laboratory notebooks bound in marbled black cardboard; and a cardboard biscuit tin containing two hundred glass marbles that they had won from the Joshi brothers in 1993.\n\nVivek picked up the wooden cricket bat.\n\nHe ran his thumb over the red insulation tape.\n\nHe remembered the summer afternoon when he had hit a tennis ball through the stained-glass fanlight above the grandfather's Sagwan door. He had been nine years old, terrified, crying so hard that he had hidden inside the grain storage bin in the pantry.\n\nIt was Jayant who had stood in front of their father.\n\n*“I hit the ball, Baba,”* the fourteen-year-old Jayant had lied, taking the reprimand, standing straight with his hands behind his back while their father lectured him on the sacred duty of respecting ancestral property.\n\nVivek looked across the cardboard box at his brother.\n\n'You took the blame for the glass,' Vivek whispered.\n\nJayant looked up from the packing tape, a faint, wry smile touching the corners of his mouth.\n\n'The glass cost fourteen rupees to replace,' Jayant said softly. 'Father made me clean the well bucket for two weeks.'\n\nHe smoothed the brown tape over the seam of the cardboard box with the palm of his hand.\n\n'Take the bat to Hyderabad, Vivek,' Jayant said. 'Show it to your boys. Tell them that their father played cricket with a bat held together by brass screws.'\n\nVivek clutched the old willow against his chest.\n\nHe did not answer. He pressed his face into the dry, sweet-smelling wood, letting the tears soak into the grain.\n\nOutside, from the minaret of the mosque near the river, the evening azan rose into the sky, interweaving with the bells of the Ganpati temple at the corner of the lane.\n\nThe city of Pune was moving into the night.\n\nDownstairs, Rohini's footsteps could be heard in the kitchen—the clatter of the brass tea strainer against the rim of a stainless steel pan, the hiss of the gas burner, the familiar, comforting domestic rhythm of a woman preparing food for living people.\n\nThe wada was doomed. On Monday morning, the iron excavators would arrive; the Sagwan door would be unhinged; the basalt stones would be carted away to fill low-lying land near the highway; the air would be filled with the dust of eighty-seven years of memory.\n\nA concrete tower would rise in its place, with aluminium sliding windows, vitrified ceramic floor tiles, intercom panels, and eight underground parking slots.\n\nPeople with no memory of Dattatreya Kulkarni would live in the air where the teak rafters had once held up the sky.\n\nThey would sleep in air-conditioned bedrooms forty feet above the stone where Malati had taken her last breath.\n\nThey would never know that two brothers had stood in this dust, holding forty green stamp papers and an iron latch, and had chosen to love each other rather than to destroy each other.\n\nJayant taped the last box shut, wrote *'BOOKS — KEEP DRY'* in black marker on the lid, and stood up.\n\nHe wiped the dust from his knees, touched Vivek's shoulder, and looked around the empty timber loft one last time.\n\n'Come, Vivek,' Jayant said into the darkness. 'Rohini has made tea. Let us go downstairs.'"
